WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.200 --> 00:00:19.031
The path of the patched Dunlop tires led us back in the direction of the school.

2
00:00:19.512 --> 00:00:23.594
Although we lost sight of the tire tracks from time to time, we usually regained it after a

3
00:00:23.654 --> 00:00:29.038
while. Whenever we sighted the tire tracks, they were almost always seen alongside the hooves of cows.

4
00:00:29.659 --> 00:00:33.553
The tracks took us to the Ragged Shaw, a small wooded area that was behind the school.

5
00:00:34.634 --> 00:00:38.297
"Well," said Holmes, "this is an interesting complication.

6
00:00:38.877 --> 00:00:43.921
It seems that this cyclist must have emerged from these woods, because the path begins and ends here."

7
00:00:44.541 --> 00:00:47.403
Holmes thoughtfully took his pipe out of his pocket and had a smoke.

8
00:00:48.043 --> 00:00:53.347
After his smoke Holmes continued, "It is, of course, possible that a cunning man might change the tires

9
00:00:53.427 --> 00:00:54.128
on his bicycle."

10
00:00:54.948 --> 00:00:55.949
I nodded in agreement.

11
00:00:57.270 --> 00:01:02.449
"A criminal capable of such a plan," continued Holmes, "is a man I should be proud to do

12
00:01:02.489 --> 00:01:03.069
business with.

13
00:01:03.730 --> 00:01:07.191
But enough of this speculation, we have still a lot to explore."

14
00:01:08.232 --> 00:01:12.874
Holmes led me to another muddy portion of the moor, and it was here that Holmes became truly

15
00:01:12.954 --> 00:01:16.076
excited. In the mud there were the tracks of Palmer tires.

16
00:01:16.096 --> 00:01:19.678
"Here is the German teacher," said Holmes happily.

17
00:01:19.698 --> 00:01:25.261
"So far our reasoning has been sound, so let's follow the tracks though I fear they won't lead

18
00:01:25.301 --> 00:01:25.921
very far."

19
00:01:26.822 --> 00:01:29.203
Once again we did our best to follow the scattered tracks.

20
00:01:29.663 --> 00:01:34.811
At one such interval, Holmes stopped and observed that the cyclist was sprinting, because his weight had been

21
00:01:34.851 --> 00:01:38.134
moved to the front wheel as if he was leaning over the handlebars.

22
00:01:39.395 --> 00:01:39.896
"Look, here!"

23
00:01:40.376 --> 00:01:41.097
exclaimed Holmes.

24
00:01:41.597 --> 00:01:42.238
"He has fallen."

25
00:01:43.099 --> 00:01:46.882
To the side of the path, there was a flattened area of bushes that had been made by

26
00:01:46.922 --> 00:01:47.803
the fall of the rider.

27
00:01:48.323 --> 00:01:51.806
On closer examination, we found that some of the bushes were covered in blood.

28
00:01:52.747 --> 00:01:54.689
"Stand clear, Watson," said Holmes.

29
00:01:54.709 --> 00:01:56.911
"We must read this very carefully.

30
00:01:57.631 --> 00:02:02.174
He fell wounded, because the blood is not where his legs or arms would have made contact with

31
00:02:02.214 --> 00:02:02.675
the ground."

32
00:02:02.695 --> 00:02:04.776
"Was he attacked by a bull?"

33
00:02:05.377 --> 00:02:05.797
I asked.

34
00:02:05.817 --> 00:02:08.319
"There are cow hoof marks on the ground."

35
00:02:09.039 --> 00:02:10.400
"Unlikely," said Holmes.

36
00:02:10.420 --> 00:02:12.621
"But you are right to note the cow hoofs.

37
00:02:13.322 --> 00:02:15.904
There are no other tracks to explain this freakish event.

38
00:02:16.424 --> 00:02:19.026
It seems he remounted his bicycle; let us continue."

39
00:02:19.986 --> 00:02:23.809
We had not gone far when the gleam of metal flickered from among the bushes and grass.

40
00:02:24.389 --> 00:02:28.112
We hurried to the spot where we found a Palmer-tired bicycle covered in blood.

41
00:02:28.936 --> 00:02:31.899
Not far from the bicycle lay a tall, bearded man on his side.

42
00:02:32.499 --> 00:02:34.220
I knew in an instant he must be dead.

43
00:02:34.861 --> 00:02:39.665
Holmes and I carefully turned him over to examine him, and that's when we discovered the horrible blow

44
00:02:39.685 --> 00:02:40.285
to his skull.

45
00:02:40.946 --> 00:02:45.810
We had no doubt we had found the German teacher, but I could tell that Holmes was troubled

46
00:02:45.830 --> 00:02:46.610
by our discovery.

47
00:02:46.630 --> 00:02:50.613
"It's a bit difficult to decide what to do," said Holmes.

48
00:02:50.633 --> 00:02:56.298
"I want to continue, because the young duke may be in danger, but, on the other hand, we

49
00:02:56.378 --> 00:02:58.660
must inform the police of our startling discovery."

50
00:02:59.892 --> 00:03:01.633
"I could take a note back," I offered.

51
00:03:01.653 --> 00:03:06.936
"No, Watson, I need Suddenly Holmes stopped talking and listened very intently.

52
00:03:06.956 --> 00:03:08.817
"Do you hear that, Watson?"

53
00:03:09.437 --> 00:03:09.958
asked Holmes.

54
00:03:09.978 --> 00:03:13.199
"I believe there is a man working behind that small hill.

55
00:03:13.980 --> 00:03:14.900
Ask him over, won't you?"

56
00:03:15.801 --> 00:03:19.023
I brought the man, and Holmes sent him with a note to the headmaster.

57
00:03:19.943 --> 00:03:24.306
Holmes took out his pipe and reviewed the facts: first, the boy had left on his own free

58
00:03:24.346 --> 00:03:30.629
will; second, the German teacher had followed the boy; third, the boy had had a companion strong enough

59
00:03:30.669 --> 00:03:35.812
to kill the German teacher; and fourth, we had found the German teacher five miles from the school,

60
00:03:36.172 --> 00:03:39.874
which meant the boy and his companion had had a fairly swift means of traveling.

61
00:03:39.894 --> 00:03:47.499
"Well," said Holmes, "we aren't any nearer to a solution, but we have followed one set of tracks

62
00:03:47.619 --> 00:03:48.239
to their end.

63
00:03:49.179 --> 00:03:51.901
Let us now see about those patched Dunlop tires."

64
00:03:52.781 --> 00:03:57.024
We picked up the tracks of the Dunlop bicycle tires, and followed them away from the school.

65
00:03:57.600 --> 00:03:59.581
The tracks led to a high bushy hill.

66
00:04:00.221 --> 00:04:02.282
On the top of the hill the path forked.

67
00:04:02.982 --> 00:04:08.025
One path lead in the direction of Holderness Hall and the other led toward a village, but there

68
00:04:08.045 --> 00:04:11.466
were no tire tracks to help us decide which way the rider had gone.

69
00:04:12.066 --> 00:04:14.327
Holmes decided to take the path toward the village.

70
00:04:15.128 --> 00:04:19.209
As we approached the village inn, Holmes stumbled, groaned, and grabbed his ankle.

71
00:04:19.229 --> 00:04:22.191
"Give me your shoulder, Watson," said Holmes.

72
00:04:22.771 --> 00:04:24.892
I supported my friend and led him toward the inn.

73
00:04:26.408 --> 00:04:30.671
An elderly man sat near the front of the inn smoking his pipe, and Holmes greeted him.

74
00:04:31.592 --> 00:04:32.372
"How are you, Mr.

75
00:04:32.392 --> 00:04:33.033
Reuben Hayes?"

76
00:04:33.593 --> 00:04:39.157
The man was a bit surprised, and asked suspiciously, "Who are you and how do you know my

77
00:04:39.217 --> 00:04:43.320
name?" "It's printed on the sign above your head," said Holmes.

78
00:04:43.340 --> 00:04:47.283
"Do you, perhaps, have a carriage in your stable?"

79
00:04:47.303 --> 00:04:49.204
"No," said the man coldly.

80
00:04:49.224 --> 00:04:52.666
"I've sprained my ankle," explained Holmes.

81
00:04:52.686 --> 00:04:54.568
"I can barely walk."

82
00:04:55.516 --> 00:04:58.218
"So hop," said the man ungraciously.

83
00:04:58.918 --> 00:05:02.641
The man was obviously hostile, but Holmes continued to ask him questions.

84
00:05:03.181 --> 00:05:05.443
Holmes asked about a bicycle, but he had none.

85
00:05:06.183 --> 00:05:10.847
Holmes mentioned that the police had been informed about the duke's son, and the man became agitated.

86
00:05:11.547 --> 00:05:15.110
He asked about the news, and Holmes said there had been a telegram from Liverpool.

87
00:05:16.671 --> 00:05:17.771
The man seemed to relax.

88
00:05:18.212 --> 00:05:22.014
He then informed us that he had once worked for the duke, but had been fired.

89
00:05:22.595 --> 00:05:28.226
Holmes sympathized with him, and said, "I understand your hard feelings, but can you help me get to

90
00:05:28.266 --> 00:05:28.606
the Hall?"

91
00:05:28.626 --> 00:05:32.348
"Aye, my two horses can take you to the Hall.

92
00:05:32.949 --> 00:05:36.650
I hate the duke, but I bear no ill feeling toward his son.

93
00:05:37.091 --> 00:05:38.712
I am glad he's safe in Liverpool."

94
00:05:38.732 --> 00:05:42.834
"Before we go, we'll have some dinner," said Holmes.

95
00:05:43.714 --> 00:05:45.355
The man took Holmes' money and left.

96
00:05:45.915 --> 00:05:48.697
Once the man had left, I was amazed at Holmes' quick recovery.

97
00:05:49.117 --> 00:05:51.318
His ankle showed no sign of being sprained.

98
00:05:51.338 --> 00:05:52.379
"Come, Watson.

99
00:05:52.599 --> 00:05:54.140
There is much for us to discover here.

100
00:05:54.676 --> 00:05:56.399
This man is certainly more than he seems.

101
00:05:57.040 --> 00:05:59.043
We need to take a look at those horses he mentioned."
